{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261026T035441Z-69d65fae", "ts": "2026-10-26T03:58:37Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev", "branch": "feature/733-consumer-digest"}, "body": {"base_commit": "4d544b03f2b523077d34ac2a7209f5976848a909", "base_tree": "b25c2f15b70299d2591f4ed270247b8720f1b194", "head_tree": "139d9400f90accc578c9cbcc08ed163e0f01a807", "head_commit": null, "dispatch_commit": "4d544b03f2b523077d34ac2a7209f5976848a909", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "plan-override: final", "stage": "inner", "tier": "standard", "roster": [{"role": "reviewer", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/733-design.md", "plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "The .git/.prawduct refusal in _validated_path compares spellings, not identity, so it fails open on a case-insensitive filesystem", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the deny-list is checked as `inside[0] in (\".git\", \".prawduct\")`, a case-sensitive string comparison of the first path component, but `Path.resolve()` does not canonicalise case on macOS (APFS default) or Windows. So `path: .Git/config` or `path: .PRAWDUCT/x` resolves inside the repo, passes the check, and names the real `.git/config`. Design Section 1 and requirements line 159 both say a path resolving under `.git/` is refused. Once Chunk 04 wires this in, the cut inserts fragment-authored text into that file. With `heading: \"## {version}\"`, `layout: paragraphs`, `entry: \"{note}\"` (all valid) the inserted span is `## v3.8.0` (a git-config comment), a blank line, then the note verbatim. A note reading `[core]` / `fsmonitor = <cmd>` is then valid git config, and git runs it on the operator's next `git status`. A PR that changes project-state.yaml and adds a fragment can set this up. This is the hook-surface learning \"A VALIDATOR refusing only the malformed can still fail OPEN\" coming back. Fix it with one construction, not another spelling. Compare every component of the resolved repo-relative path casefolded (`any(p.casefold() == \".git\" for p in parts)`; `.prawduct` as the first component, casefolded), or compare by identity (`os.path.samefile` against `root/.git` and `root/.prawduct` and their descendants). A `.git` component below the top level (a vendored nested clone's `vendor/x/.git/config`) is in the same class. Pin it with a test that does not depend on the host filesystem's case sensitivity: `.GIT/CHANGELOG.md`, `.Prawduct/CHANGELOG.md` and `sub/.git/config` each refused with \"must not write\".", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py"], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "paragraphs layout rstrips every note line, where the design says verbatim and CD11 says byte-identical", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `_entry_lines` applies `line.rstrip()` to every layout. Design Section 4's table says a `paragraphs` continuation is \"verbatim (today's form)\", and today's `render_consumer_section` keeps a note's internal trailing whitespace. A note using a Markdown hard break (two trailing spaces) would therefore render differently after Chunk 04, breaking CD11 for that input and dropping the line break in the published changelog. The golden fixture has no trailing whitespace, so it cannot catch this. Either keep paragraphs lines verbatim, or record the departure. Add a paragraphs case with a trailing-space line either way. Would be a WARNING at boundary.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "test evidence is stale for this tree", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1 (evidence predates the session; its evidence_tree is not this tree). The new module tests, the golden, and both write_all_or_none tests have no recorded run on this tree, so the suite is owed before this chunk is committed.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "the module docstring's reach and purity claims are not true of this tree", "recommendation": "\"``change_cut`` writes and ``release_readiness`` advises, and both ask this module\" is present tense, but nothing imports consumer_digest until Chunks 03 and 04. \"Everything here is pure except read_declaration and read_changelog\" is also wrong: `parse_declaration` calls `_validated_path`, which calls `resolve()` on the filesystem, and the symlink test depends on that. Make the purity claim relational (name `parse_declaration` as touching the filesystem through path resolution) or delete it. Prose, NOTE-level.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "ChangelogText.render embeds an invisible U+FEFF literal", "recommendation": "`return (\"\ufeff\" if self.bom else \"\")` holds a raw zero-width BOM character in source. A reader sees what looks like an empty string, and an editor or formatter that strips BOMs turns it into one silently. The round-trip test would then go red, but only after the damage is done. Spell it `\"\\ufeff\"`, as the tests do.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "the golden fixture's shape differs from the plan and design descriptions", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's description and the design's CD11 test-plan row describe two fragments (one `none:`), a `## v9.9.9` section and `## v9.9.8` below. The test uses three fragments and `## v3.8.0` / `## v3.7.1`, because `_project` hard-code